/* 1000FiX ERP User Guide — authored knowledge map (areas + 67 modules).
   Everything measurable comes from _catalog.json (extracted from the live nav payload);
   everything written here is the human explanation layered on top. */
'use strict';

const AREAS = {
  A01: { intro: 'Your personalised starting page — the Role Centre. It gathers the KPIs, exceptions and shortcuts that matter to YOUR job on one screen.', biz: 'Every role in an ERP sees a different home page. This is the "role centre" pattern used by Dynamics 365: a service agent sees intake queues, an accountant sees unposted journals and VAT deadlines, a technician sees today\u2019s jobs.', b: ['Start each day from one screen instead of hunting through menus', 'Exceptions are surfaced for you — no report to run'] },
  A02: { intro: 'The heart of 1000 FiX — receiving, dispatching, repairing and returning customer devices across branches, field visits and the workshop.', biz: 'After-sales service lives or dies on three numbers: SLA compliance (did we respond in the promised time), TAT — turnaround time (how long the device was with us), and First-Time-Fix rate (repairs completed without a repeat fault). Every module in this area feeds those numbers. In Bangladesh, device custody documentation also matters: a signed job sheet at intake is the legal proof of what the customer handed over.', b: ['One order object carries a device from first call to delivery', 'Custody, diagnosis, estimate, approval and QC are captured as structured steps — not paper notes', 'Dispatch balances skill, zone and travel time'] },
  A03: { intro: 'Everything customer-facing before and after the repair: the customer database, sales pipeline, loyalty, complaints, the 16743 hotline, Bengali chatbot and campaigns.', biz: 'A "party master" keeps one identity per customer across phone, walk-in, e-commerce and B2B accounts — duplicates destroy loyalty points and warranty lookups. The hotline 16743 is the company\u2019s front door; its queue, IVR and callback discipline directly set perceived service quality. CSAT after each job and NPS per quarter are the standard experience measures.', b: ['One customer, one record — history, warranty and loyalty always visible', 'Complaints are tracked to closure with root-cause codes, feeding ISO 9001 corrective actions', 'The Bengali chatbot deflects routine "where is my device?" calls'] },
  A04: { intro: 'How services are priced, sold and contracted: catalogue, rate cards, quotations and tenders, AMC contracts, warranty, subscriptions, e-commerce, marketplace and partners.', biz: 'AMC — Annual Maintenance Contract — is the recurring-revenue backbone of after-sales businesses: fixed yearly fee, defined response SLAs, periodic visits. quotations in Bangladesh B2B often go through formal tender submission with BOQ (bill of quantities). Warranty terms (labour vs parts, in-warranty vs out) decide who pays for a repair — the customer, 1000 FiX, or the manufacturer.', b: ['Price consistency: the same service cannot be quoted two different amounts', 'Contract SLAs and visit entitlements are enforced by the system, not memory', 'Warranty checks at intake stop revenue leakage on free repairs'] },
  A05: { intro: 'Circular-economy business lines: device rental / Device-as-a-Service, trade-in and refurbishment, and certified e-waste recycling.', biz: 'Extended producer responsibility and e-waste rules in Bangladesh (Environment Conservation Rules) make certified disposal a compliance issue, not just ethics. Refurbishment margin depends on graded inventory: A/B/C grade devices carry different warranties and prices. Rental fleets live on utilisation rate and damage recovery.', b: ['Fleet assets and their revenue contracts are linked — utilisation is visible per device', 'Trade-in value, refurb cost and resale margin are captured per unit', 'Recycling certificates and chain-of-custody records satisfy auditors'] },
  A06: { intro: 'Spare parts and materials: inventory across branches and central warehouse, procurement, suppliers, demand planning and import compliance.', biz: 'Parts availability is the #1 cause of failed first-time fixes. Critical spares carry min/max levels and reorder points; slow movers tie up working capital. Import compliance in Bangladesh means LC (letter of credit) terms, HS codes, customs assessment and VAT on imports — errors here strand shipments for weeks.', b: ['Stock visible across all branches in real time — no more "call the warehouse"', 'Reorder alerts fire before a critical part stocks out', 'Landed cost including duty and freight rolls into part cost automatically'] },
  A07: { intro: 'The physical backbone: customer device registry, company assets and test equipment, fleet, branch infrastructure and IoT telemetry.', biz: 'Serialised tracking means each device is an individual record with warranty, repair history and owner — the basis of every "when was it bought?" answer. Test equipment itself needs calibration cycles for ISO 17025-style credibility. Fleet maintenance and branch facility upkeep (generators, UPS, HVAC) keep service delivery running.', b: ['Any device\u2019s full history in two clicks — intake, repairs, parts, owner', 'Calibration and maintenance due dates for test gear never slip', 'Predictive telemetry turns break-fix into planned maintenance'] },
  A08: { intro: 'Money: general ledger, receivables and invoicing, payables and expenses, treasury and banking, Bangladesh tax, revenue recognition, fixed assets and FP&A.', biz: 'In Bangladesh every taxable supply needs a Mushak 6.3 tax invoice and the month\u2019s VAT position is filed as a Mushak 9.1 return to NBR. Payments ride mobile financial services — bKash and Nagad — alongside cards and bank transfers; each rail reconciles differently. Revenue for AMC contracts is earned monthly even though cash arrives yearly — that is revenue recognition.', b: ['One invoice per job, with VAT computed by rule — not by calculator', 'Bank, bKash and Nagad statements reconcile daily', 'Month-end close is a checklist, not a fire drill'] },
  A09: { intro: 'People: HR lifecycle, attendance and rostering, payroll with statutory deductions, performance and incentives, learning, recruitment, and health & safety.', biz: 'Technician certification levels drive both dispatch quality and career progression — an uncertified tech must not be dispatched to a warranty job. Bangladesh payroll deducts provident fund, tax at source and adjusts for festivals (festival bonus is statutory). Field rostering must respect labour-hour rules and travel time.', b: ['Rosters, leave and attendance feed payroll without re-typing', 'Incentives compute from system data — jobs closed, CSAT, upsell', 'Skill certificates expire with alerts — no expired-certificate dispatch'] },
  A10: { intro: 'Trust: ISO 9001 quality management, governance and risk, documents with e-signature, and the technical knowledge library.', biz: '1000 FiX is Bangladesh\u2019s only ISO 9001:2015 certified IT service provider — that certificate is audited yearly and depends on documented processes, corrective actions (CAPA) and management review records existing in the system. E-signature (Digital Signature Act 2018 context) makes job sheets and approvals legally dependable.', b: ['Non-conformances raised on the shop floor flow into CAPA with owners and deadlines', 'Audit-ready evidence: every document versioned, signed and retrievable', 'Fix knowledge lives in a library, not in senior technicians\u2019 heads'] },
  A11: { intro: 'Insight: BI dashboards, the AI/ML model portfolio, the governed data platform, and process automation (RPA-style jobs).', biz: 'Dashboards fail without governed data — one metric definition, one owner. The 14-model AI portfolio ranges from dispatch prediction to churn scoring; models are only as trusted as their data lineage. Automation pays off first in high-volume clerical steps: invoice assembly, dunning messages, report distribution.', b: ['Every KPI on a dashboard has one agreed definition — arguments end', 'Forecasts (demand, dispatch load) are produced weekly without analyst effort', 'Repetitive clicks are automated; people handle exceptions'] },
  A12: { intro: 'The foundation: integrations and APIs, identity and security, platform administration, master data, multi-entity localisation, internal IT, and projects.', biz: 'An ERP is a nervous system — it must talk to bKash/Nagad, courier APIs, e-commerce and government portals through a governed integration layer. Role-based access means a branch cashier sees cash screens, not GL journals. Multi-entity prepares the group for subsidiaries with separate books but shared masters.', b: ['New integrations plug in through one pattern — no point-to-point spaghetti', 'Every action is attributable to a named user (audit trail)', 'Group growth (new entity, new country) doesn\u2019t mean a new ERP'] }
};

const MODULES = {
  /* A02 Service Operations */
  M01: { p: 'Turns every customer request — hotline, web, app, branch walk-in or B2B feed — into one tracked service order with device custody, diagnosis, estimate, approval, repair and QC stages.', how: ['Open the Order Operations Board (a dashboard) to see live WIP by stage and branch', 'Create a new order from the Omni-Channel Intake screen — channel, customer, device serial and stated fault', 'Use All Service Orders (grid) with filters: branch, stage, SLA state, age', 'Open any order — the record page shows the 8-step process bar (Received → Diagnosing → Estimate → Approved → In Progress → QC → Ready → Delivered); click a stage to move it', 'Close the loop with SLA Compliance Report and Order Cycle-Time (TAT) Distribution'], biz: 'The service order is the legal and operational spine: custody started when the customer signed intake, estimate approval protects margin, QC gates repeat faults. The 12 intake channels ensure nothing arrives "off the books".', b: 'Nothing is lost between phone call and delivery — every device is always in a known stage with a known owner.' },
  M02: { p: 'Assigns field jobs to the right technician based on skill, zone and travel, and tracks them to completion on the dispatch board.', how: ['Open the Dispatch Board — columns are zones (Dhanmondi, Mirpur, Uttara…), cards are open work orders with tech and travel minutes', 'Drag/click-assign a card: the Candidate Scoring panel ranks technicians by skill match, load and distance', 'Track status in real time (EN ROUTE → ON SITE → DONE) as technicians update from their mobile portal', 'Review the day in the Dispatch Performance by Zone and Travel-Time reports'], biz: 'Dispatch quality = first-time-fix + travel cost. Skills-based assignment avoids the classic "wrong tech, second visit" failure; zone columns expose imbalance at a glance.', b: 'The right technician reaches the right customer first time — fewer repeat visits, lower fuel cost, happier customers.' },
  M31: { p: 'Runs the central workshop: bench repair orders, workshop capacity, technician load and internal QC for jobs too complex for field or branch.', how: ['Create a Workshop Repair Order when a device is escalated to bench level', 'Use Bench & Board views to see jobs per bench and per technician', 'Move devices through Receive → Diagnose → Await Parts → Repair → QC stages, mirroring the physical bench card', 'Review Repair Cycle Time by Level and the Internal Rework Loop weekly'], biz: 'Depot repair is batch-efficient but risky for queueing — WIP ageing per bench exposes stuck devices before customers complain. Rework rate measures internal quality.', b: 'No device sleeps on a bench unnoticed — ageing and rework are visible and owned.' },
  M03: { p: 'Manages pickup and delivery logistics: consignments, multi-leg journeys (customer → branch → workshop → back) and courier integration.', how: ['Create a consignment (5 types: field pickup, branch transfer, workshop send, return, direct delivery)', 'Track legs on the journey board — each leg has a carrier, status and proof', 'Reconcile courier invoices against delivered legs', 'Monitor Consignment Cycle Time and Courier Performance (Pathao/RedX/Steadfast)'], biz: 'Pick & drop is a promise: "we collect in 2 hours". Multi-leg visibility prevents the classic loss point — the device that made it to the courier but never checked in at the workshop.', b: 'Every device in transit has a live location and proof of each handover — zero "where is it?" escalations.' },
  M04: { p: 'Digitises the branch experience: token queue, counter workstation, cash desk and end-of-day cash declaration.', how: ['Walk-in customers take a token (kiosk or desk) — the Token & Waiting Display shows live wait per counter', 'Counter staff use the Counter Workstation screen: serve token → find customer → create/receive order', 'The cash desk records payments (cash, card, bKash, Nagad) via Payment Capture', 'End of day: the Shift Close screen balances the cash drawer against the system — discrepancies need a reason code'], biz: 'Token discipline converts a crowd into a measurable queue: average wait and serve times become numbers you manage. Daily shift-close balancing with reason-coded variance is basic retail hygiene and an ISO control.', b: 'Branches run the same way every day; cash differences are caught same-evening, not month-end.' },
  M05: { p: 'Remote support sessions and the managed-device estate: remote diagnosis, health scoring and preventive interventions.', how: ['Open a remote session from a service order — tools and session notes are logged', 'Monitor the estate dashboard: devices by health score and risk', 'Push preventive actions (cleanup, updates) to amber devices before they turn red', 'Review Remote Resolution Rate and MSP SLA Attainment by Client — every remote fix is a field visit saved'], biz: 'MSP economics: a resolved-remote session costs a fraction of a field visit. Health scoring from telemetry (disk, temperature, uptime) predicts failures; fixing remotely is the margin.', b: 'Many problems disappear without a truck roll — customers faster served, technicians freed for real repairs.' },
  M06: { p: 'Specialist data services: recovery, migration, secure erasure and forensic handling with chain of custody.', how: ['Create a data-service case with the 7 specialism types (recovery, migration, wipe…)', 'Record media condition and chain-of-custody events at every handover', 'Track the case through evaluation → attempt → result with customer approval gates', 'Issue the completion certificate from the case'], biz: 'Data work is high-value and high-liability: chain of custody protects against "you lost my photos" disputes; secure erasure follows standards because certificates are audited.', b: 'High-value jobs carry zero ambiguity about what was received, attempted and returned.' },
  /* A03 Customer Experience */
  M33: { p: 'The single source of truth for every person and organisation 1000 FiX deals with — customers, prospects, suppliers-as-parties, staff-as-users.', how: ['Search the party register (grid) by phone, NID, BIN or name — duplicates are blocked at save', 'Open a party record: roles (customer/employee/partner), communication defaults, KYC status', 'Maintain account hierarchies for B2B groups (holding company → branches)', 'Review new/merged parties in the data-steward queue'], biz: 'One party, many roles. Phone-number dedup is the #1 defence against double loyalty accounts and split histories. B2B hierarchies roll up revenue and AMC coverage correctly.', b: 'Anyone can answer "who is this customer and what is our history?" in seconds.' },
  M07: { p: 'The sales pipeline: leads, qualification, opportunities, quotes and won/lost analysis for B2B service contracts.', how: ['Capture a lead (web form, event, call) and qualify it against the scoring card', 'Convert qualified leads to opportunities with stage, value and expected close', 'Attach a quotation (from M36) and track the deal through the pipeline board', 'Analyse conversion and cycle-time by source and owner'], biz: 'Pipeline discipline — stage definitions and exit criteria — is what makes revenue forecasts credible. Lead source attribution shows which channel actually produces contracts.', b: 'Sales leaders see true coverage of the target; nothing sits forgotten in a notebook.' },
  M10: { p: 'Loyalty programme: tiers, enrolment, points ledger, benefit engine and redemption.', how: ['Enrol a customer at intake — tier starts at Base', 'Points accrue automatically from completed, paid orders; the ledger shows every earn/burn line', 'Redeem points at the cash desk as a payment line', 'Run the liability and breakage report for finance'], biz: 'Points are a balance-sheet liability — finance must know the outstanding balance. Tier benefits (priority queue, discount rates) are contractual promises the engine enforces consistently.', b: 'Repeat customers are recognised automatically — retention becomes a system behaviour, not a memory game.' },
  M34: { p: 'Experience measurement and complaint management: surveys, CSAT/NPS/CES, complaint cases to closure with root cause.', how: ['Surveys fire automatically after job closure (SMS/portal) — CSAT, NPS and CES questions', 'Review the experience dashboard: score trends by branch, service line and technician', 'Log a complaint case, classify it, assign an owner and drive it to closure with root-cause coding', 'Feed systemic root causes into M23 CAPA'], biz: 'CSAT after every job, NPS quarterly — the industry-standard pair. A complaint closed without root cause will repeat; root-cause taxonomy makes month-over-month improvement measurable.', b: 'Bad experiences are caught and fixed before they become reviews; good ones are recognised.' },
  M27: { p: 'The 16743 contact centre: IVR and queue design, agent desktop, callbacks and channel (voice/chat/email/social) unification.', how: ['Agents work the unified queue — calls, chats and emails arrive in one list with customer context', 'Use the agent desktop: identify party → see open orders → create or update cases in one screen', 'Manage IVR flows and callback lists from the supervisor view', 'Monitor live SLA (answer time, abandonment) on the wallboard'], biz: 'Hotline 16743 is the brand. Answer-time SLA and abandonment are the two numbers a contact centre lives by; unified queue stops channel-silo blindness.', b: 'Callers reach a person quickly, and that person already sees the caller\u2019s history.' },
  M32: { p: 'The Bengali/English conversational AI that deflects routine contacts and books services 24/7.', how: ['Deploy flows: order status, warranty check, booking, FAQ — trained on Bengali, English and mixed input', 'Complex intents hand off to M27 with the transcript attached', 'Review unresolved utterances weekly to grow the training set', 'Track deflection rate and containment score'], biz: 'Bangladeshi customers mix Bangla and English mid-sentence — the intent engine is trained for it. Every deflected "status check" call is agent capacity returned to complex cases.', b: 'Customers get instant answers at 2 a.m. in Bangla; agents handle humans, not lookups.' },
  M35: { p: 'Campaign management: plan, audience-build from segments, execute across SMS/email/portal, measure.', how: ['Plan a campaign with objective, budget and window', 'Build the audience from CRM segments (e.g., AMC expiring in 30 days)', 'Launch and monitor delivery/engagement', 'Attribute resulting orders to the campaign code'], biz: 'Campaign ROI only exists with attribution — the campaign code on the resulting order closes the loop. Segments computed from service history (e.g., 3-year-old devices) outperform cold lists.', b: 'Marketing spend converts measurably into service orders.' },
  /* A04 Commercial & Revenue */
  M37: { p: 'The catalogue of everything sellable: devices, spare parts, service definitions and bundles, with bilingual descriptions.', how: ['Maintain items in the Unified Item Master with typed taxonomy (device/part/service)', 'Define service templates: included diagnostics, SLA, default duration', 'Publish bundles (e.g., AMC + rental printer) and price links to M11', 'Control release status — draft items are invisible to quotes until released'], biz: 'Catalogue discipline is why quotes and invoices agree: everything billable is an item with a price hook. Typed taxonomy prevents "is it a part or a service?" arguments at invoicing.', b: 'Whatever was sold is exactly what gets billed — no mismatch, no leakage.' },
  M11: { p: 'Pricing engine: price books by customer tier/market, rate cards for labour, approval workflow for exceptions.', how: ['Maintain price books per segment (retail, corporate, government) with effective dates', 'Keep technician rate cards (by certification level and hour type)', 'Quote with list price; discounts beyond threshold route to the approval flow', 'Audit price changes with who/when/why'], biz: 'Price governance protects both margin and fairness — the same service at the same tier should cost the same in Dhanmondi and Chattogram. Effective-dated books preserve historical invoice accuracy.', b: 'Frontline staff quote confidently; margin leaks through unapproved discounts stop.' },
  M36: { p: 'Quotations and tender management: structured quotes, revisions, BOQ and tender submission packages.', how: ['Create a quotation from an opportunity or walk-in; lines pull catalogue prices', 'Revise — every version is kept; the customer-facing PDF is versioned', 'For tenders: assemble the BOQ, compliance documents and technical response', 'Convert a won quote to a contract (M08) or order in one click'], biz: 'B2B deals are won on response speed and compliance. Version-controlled revisions answer "which price did we send them?" instantly; tender packages mirror government submission formats.', b: 'Quotes go out in hours with zero re-keying into orders afterwards.' },
  M08: { p: 'AMC contract lifecycle: templated contracts, entitlements, periodic visit scheduling, renewals and escalations.', how: ['Create an AMC from a template: coverage lines, response SLA, visit entitlements', 'The system schedules periodic visits (quarterly PM) automatically', 'Entitlement checks at intake validate coverage before free service is promised', 'Renewal alerts fire 60/30/7 days before expiry into M07 pipeline'], biz: 'AMC is recurring revenue with legal obligations: response-time promises and included visits are contractual. Entitlement-at-intake is where AMC value is defended — without it, every covered repair becomes a negotiation.', b: 'Renewals start months early with facts; covered customers get instant service, uncovered get a quote.' },
  M09: { p: 'Warranty register and claims: manufacturer warranty validation, 1000 FiX own repair warranty and claim recovery.', how: ['Register devices with warranty terms at sale/intake (labour vs parts, duration)', 'Every intake auto-checks warranty status — the order is flagged in/out of warranty', 'File claims to manufacturers with evidence packs (diagnosis, parts, photos)', 'Track claim recovery: submitted → approved → credited'], biz: 'Warranty decides who pays. A repair done free for an out-of-warranty device is pure margin loss; an unclaimed manufacturer warranty is money left on the table. Repair warranty (own work, e.g., 90 days) drives repeat-fault accountability.', b: 'Nobody guesses coverage — the order shows it, and recoverable claims are actually claimed.' },
  M39: { p: 'Subscriptions and recurring billing: subscription objects, schedules, proration, dunning.', how: ['Create a subscription (per device, per user or per site) from the catalogue', 'Billing schedules generate automatically — monthly/quarterly, with proration on mid-cycle changes', 'Dunning escalates on non-payment (reminder → hold → suspend)', 'Recognise revenue monthly via the M53 bridge'], biz: 'Subscription economics = MRR and churn. Proration fairness (upgrade mid-month) prevents disputes; dunning discipline protects cash without manual chasing.', b: 'Recurring invoices go out on time and collect themselves; churn is visible per account.' },
  M38: { p: 'The e-commerce storefront: catalogue publishing, cart, online payment and order-to-ERP flow.', how: ['Publish approved catalogue items with photos and web pricing', 'Customer orders flow in as prepaid service/product orders with payment status', 'Fulfil from branch stock or service scheduling; tracking returns to the customer portal', 'Reconcile online payments (cards, bKash/Nagad web) daily'], biz: 'The storefront is a channel like the hotline — the same order object, the same SLA. Payment-before-fulfilment reduces COD risk that plagues BD e-commerce.', b: 'Online sales enter the ERP as first-class orders — no spreadsheet bridge.' },
  M12: { p: 'Marketplace and seller operations: seller onboarding with KYC, listings, commissions and payouts.', how: ['Register sellers with KYC documents and tiered agreements', 'Sellers manage listings; category teams approve', 'Orders route to sellers with commission rules computed per line', 'Payout runs net of commission monthly with statements'], biz: 'Marketplace is trust infrastructure: KYC (trade licence, BIN) keeps the platform legally clean; transparent commission maths keeps sellers. Payout accuracy is the retention lever.', b: 'Sellers self-serve; the platform earns commission without manual ledger-keeping.' },
  M40: { p: 'Partner and channel management: partner master, recruitment, tiering, deal registration and partner portals.', how: ['Onboard partners with agreements and tier criteria', 'Partners register deals for protection and margin benefits', 'Track partner-sourced pipeline and delivered revenue', 'Score partners quarterly (revenue, certification, CSAT) for tier moves'], biz: 'Channel scale = partner economics. Deal registration prevents channel conflict — the killer of partner trust. Certification-linked tiers push partners to train, which protects the brand.', b: 'Partners sell more because the system protects and rewards their effort fairly.' },
  /* A05 Circular Economy */
  M13: { p: 'Rental and Equipment-as-a-Service: fleet catalogue, rental agreements, dispatch, returns and damage recovery.', how: ['Create a rental agreement with billing cycle and included service', 'Prepare and dispatch assets (serialised) with condition reports', 'Returns are inspected; damage beyond wear is charged per policy', 'Monitor fleet utilisation and idle-asset ageing'], biz: 'EaaS shifts revenue from sale to subscription — utilisation is the profitability dial. Condition reports at handover are the difference between recoverable damage and write-off.', b: 'The fleet earns every day it is out and is recovered fast when idle.' },
  M14: { p: 'Trade-in and refurbishment: instant valuations, grading, refurb workflow and certified resale stock.', how: ['Quote a trade-in value from model/condition rules', 'Receive and grade the device (A/B/C) with photo evidence', 'Refurbishment orders track parts+labour cost per unit', 'Graded stock lists in the resale channel with warranty tiers'], biz: 'Refurb margin = resale price − (trade-in + refurb cost). Grading integrity is the brand promise of certified refurbished; cost-per-unit visibility stops loss-making trades.', b: 'Traded devices become profitable certified stock instead of drawer clutter.' },
  M18: { p: 'E-waste collection and certified recycling: collection network, chain of custody, disposition and certificates.', how: ['Log collection events (drop-off, corporate pickup)', 'Track custody through storage → triage → disposition', 'Record disposition per category (reuse/recycle/destroy) with weights', 'Issue certificates to corporate donors and file regulator returns'], biz: 'E-waste is regulated: corporate donors increasingly require certified destruction and recycling proof. Weights and dispositions summarise into compliance reporting.', b: 'Compliance revenue (corporate e-waste contracts) grows with zero audit anxiety.' },
  /* A06 Supply Chain */
  M15: { p: 'Spare-parts inventory: on-hand by branch, classes (ABC), reservations, cycle counts and reorder alerts.', how: ['Check availability across branches on the parts grid before promising a repair date', 'Reserve parts against a work order at estimate approval', 'Perform cycle counts (bin-level) — variances need reasons', 'Reorder points trigger purchase requests automatically'], biz: 'ABC classification focuses counting effort where the money is. Reservation at approval is why a promised Friday delivery survives a Thursday rush.', b: 'Techs fix same-day because the part was there — and finance knows exactly what stock is worth.' },
  M41: { p: 'Warehouse management: receiving, put-away, picking, packing and warehouse transfers with bin locations.', how: ['Receive against a purchase or transfer order — GRN with inspection status', 'Put-away to bins follows system-directed suggestions', 'Pick waves for branch replenishment and workshop demand', 'Trace every movement in the bin history'], biz: 'Bin-level discipline converts "the part exists somewhere" into "the part is in A-12-3". Traceability supports both audit and recall scenarios.', b: 'Warehouse staff find things first time; stock accuracy stays above 98%.' },
  M16: { p: 'Procurement and sourcing: purchase requisitions, RFQs, purchase orders, goods receipt and invoice matching.', how: ['Raise a purchase requisition (auto from reorder or manual) with approvals', 'Run RFQs to shortlisted suppliers; compare and award', 'Issue the PO with delivery terms', 'Three-way match: PO ↔ GRN ↔ invoice before payment'], biz: 'The three-way match is the anti-fraud backbone of procurement — pay only for what was ordered and received. RFQ discipline keeps supplier pricing honest.', b: 'Buying is transparent and matched; duplicate or short-paid invoices become impossible.' },
  M42: { p: 'Supplier relationship: profiles, segmentation, qualification/audits, scorecards and compliance documents.', how: ['Maintain supplier profiles with trade licence, BIN, bank and contacts', 'Track qualification status and audit findings before awarding', 'Scorecards compute monthly: OTIF delivery, quality rejects, price variance', 'Review the supplier council pack quarterly'], biz: 'A single dodgy parts supplier destroys first-time-fix. Scorecards turn vendor conversations from opinion into data; document expiry alerts prevent buying from lapsed licences.', b: 'Better suppliers win, weak ones improve or exit — parts reliability rises every quarter.' },
  M43: { p: 'Demand planning: forecasts for parts and services, seasonality, safety stock and plan-vs-actual.', how: ['Review the statistical forecast (history + seasonality) per part/service', 'Adjust with market intelligence (new model launches, monsoon patterns)', 'Generate the supply plan and safety-stock proposals', 'Track forecast accuracy (MAPE) monthly'], biz: 'Monsoon spikes, Eid surges and new device launches are predictable — planning converts panic buying into scheduled procurement. Forecast accuracy itself is measured so the planner improves.', b: 'Right stock, right time, less working capital sitting on shelves.' },
  M44: { p: 'Import and trade compliance: LC management, HS classification, customs documentation and duty calculation.', biz: 'Import errors cost weeks: wrong HS code = mis-assessment = demurrage. LC terms and amendment tracking keep bank and supplier aligned. Duty + AIT + VAT-at-import roll into landed cost.', how: ['Open an import file with LC details and supplier invoice', 'Classify lines with HS codes; compute duty/VAT payable', 'Track customs assessment, clearance and release', 'Landed costs post to inventory value'], b: 'Shipments clear fast and parts carry their true cost — no month-end duty surprises.' },
  /* A07 Assets & Facilities */
  M17: { p: 'The customer device registry: serialised devices, ownership, warranty and full service history.', how: ['Register devices at sale or first intake (IMEI/serial, model, purchase proof)', 'Every service order links to the device record — history builds automatically', 'Look up any device by IMEI/serial from Tell-ME — owner, warranty, past repairs, trade-in status', 'Flag devices: stolen-list, do-not-service, recalled'], biz: 'Serialised registry is what makes warranty, trade-in and theft checks instant. "Do-not-service" flags protect against servicing stolen devices — a real BD market problem.', b: 'Two clicks answer the eternal question: whose device is this and what has been done to it?' },
  M45: { p: 'Company assets and test equipment: asset register, maintenance/calibration schedules and downtime tracking.', how: ['Maintain the asset register with custodian and location', 'Calibration schedules alert before due dates; results are recorded as pass/fail', 'Breakdowns log downtime and root cause', 'Depreciation flows to M49'], biz: 'Test gear out of calibration invalidates the QC step built on it — scheduling is a quality control, not admin. Downtime tracking justifies replacement decisions with data.', b: 'Equipment is always inspection-ready; auditors find calibration certificates instantly.' },
  M46: { p: 'Fleet and vehicles: vehicle register, trips, fuel, maintenance and driver compliance.', how: ['Maintain vehicles with documents (fitness, insurance, tax token) and expiry alerts', 'Log trips against work orders — distance and purpose', 'Fuel logs reconcile against distance (mileage outlier flags)', 'Service and repair schedules per vehicle'], biz: 'Field-service fuel is a top controllable cost; mileage anomalies expose both leakage and driving behaviour. Document expiry alerts keep vehicles legally on the road.', b: 'The fleet runs legally and cheaply, with costs attributed to the jobs it served.' },
  M47: { p: 'Facility and branch infrastructure: sites, leases, utilities, AMCs for building systems and maintenance tickets.', how: ['Each branch is a site record with lease terms and rent schedule', 'Building systems (generator, UPS, HVAC, security) carry their own maintenance plans', 'Facility tickets (broken AC, power issue) route to the right vendor/internal team', 'Statutory compliance items (fire safety) track certificates'], biz: 'Branch uptime is service uptime — a branch without power serves nobody. Lease and utilities data feed the true cost-per-branch in FP&A.', b: 'Buildings are managed like assets: planned upkeep, no surprise expiries.' },
  M48: { p: 'IoT telemetry and predictive maintenance: stream registry, sensor ingestion, health rules and predictions.', how: ['Onboard telemetry streams (fleet trackers, UPS sensors, customer devices with consent)', 'Rules engine watches thresholds (temperature, battery health, vibration)', 'Breaches raise predictive work orders before failure', 'Dashboards show estate health and avoided failures'], biz: 'Predictive maintenance flips cost from breakdown (overtime, expediting, angry customer) to planned visit. Consent and purpose-limitation on telemetry keep data-protection posture clean.', b: 'Fix it before it breaks — fewer emergencies, better margin.' },
  /* A08 Finance & Treasury */
  M19: { p: 'The general ledger and financial accounting core: chart of accounts, journals, subledgers and period close.', how: ['Journals post from subledgers (sales, purchases, payroll) — manual journals are the exception', 'Review unposted/rejected journals in the close checklist', 'Trial balance and financial statements by entity and period', 'Period-end: close checklist gates the lock'], biz: 'One COA with dimensions (branch, service line, project) powers every report downstream. The subledger-posting model means operations never touch GL directly — auditability by design.', b: 'Books are always reconcileable; closing is a routine, not an archaeology project.' },
  M20: { p: 'Receivables: invoicing with Mushak 6.3, collections, dunning, credit control and receipts.', how: ['Invoices assemble from completed orders/contracts — Mushak 6.3 renders automatically', 'Receipts match to invoices (bank, bKash, Nagad, cash) via the cash desk or bank feed', 'Dunning escalates by aging bucket', 'Credit holds stop new orders for overdue B2B accounts'], biz: 'In Bangladesh every taxable supply must carry a Mushak 6.3 tax invoice; errors invite VAT audit pain. Mobile-wallet reconciliation (bKash/Nagad settlement files) is a daily control.', b: 'Invoices are right the first time; cash is matched the day it lands.' },
  M52: { p: 'Payables and employee expenses: supplier invoices, payment proposals, employee claims and reimbursements.', how: ['Supplier invoices enter the three-way match queue', 'Approved invoices flow to weekly payment proposals (due-date ordered)', 'Employees submit expense claims with receipts attached — policy checks flag limits', 'Reimbursements pay with payroll or bank run'], biz: 'Paying on due dates (not early, not late) is free working capital. Policy-automated expense checks end receipt-packet theatre.', b: 'Suppliers are paid exactly on time; staff are reimbursed without chasing.' },
  M50: { p: 'Treasury and banking: bank accounts, transfers, reconciliations, petty cash and payment-rail operations.', how: ['Daily bank/wallet statement import → auto-match to receipts/payments', 'Unmatched items queue for treasury with aging', 'Fund transfers between accounts carry approval trails', 'Petty cash at branches counts via M04 declarations'], biz: 'Daily reconciliation is the strongest anti-fraud control in cash-heavy BD operations — differences surface in 24 hours, not at audit. Wallet settlement timing (bKash T+1) is managed as liquidity.', b: 'Every taka is accounted for daily; treasury knows today\u2019s true position.' },
  M51: { p: 'Bangladesh tax and regulatory: VAT determination (15% standard, specials), Mushak registers, withholding and returns.', how: ['VAT determination runs at invoice line level — standard/reduced/exempt by item and customer type', 'Mushak 6.3 (sales challan) and purchase VAT registers build continuously', 'Withholding VAT/AIT on supplier and rent payments computes at payment', 'Month-end: the Mushak 9.1 return draft assembles output-vs-input VAT with working papers'], biz: 'Under the VAT & SD Act 2012, monthly Mushak 9.1 filing with NBR is mandatory for registered persons; the registers must reconcile to the return. Correct input-VAT capture ( Mushak 6.3 from suppliers) is real money recovered.', b: 'VAT returns take hours, are audit-defensible, and input VAT is never lost.' },
  M53: { p: 'Revenue recognition and settlement: deferred revenue for AMC/subscriptions, milestone revenue and cost matching.', how: ['Contract lines schedule revenue by period (AMC monthly, subscription per cycle)', 'Deferred revenue balance reports show the obligation at any date', 'Milestone projects (installs) recognise on completion sign-off', 'Settlement allocates receipts across contracts (oldest-first or specific)'], biz: 'Cash ≠ revenue: a yearly AMC paid in January earns revenue monthly. IFRS 15-style schedules keep reported profit honest and auditors calm.', b: 'Finance reports earned revenue truthfully without spreadsheets on the side.' },
  M49: { p: 'Fixed assets and capex: asset capitalisation, depreciation schedules, revaluation and disposal.', how: ['Capex requests route with approvals before purchase', 'Assets capitalise automatically from the PO/receipt (linked to M45 records)', 'Depreciation posts monthly per method/class', 'Disposals compute gain/loss and release the asset'], biz: 'Depreciation policy consistency matters at audit; linking physical assets (M45) to financial assets (M49) ends the "two different asset lists" problem.', b: 'Capex is controlled before the money moves; asset books match reality.' },
  M54: { p: 'Costing, budget and FP&A: cost centres, standard costs, budgeting, forecasts and board reporting.', how: ['Cost centres mirror the branch/department structure', 'Standard part costs and labour rates feed job costing — actual vs standard variances surface', 'Annual budget loads by cost centre and GL line; monthly actuals compare automatically', 'Rolling forecasts and the monthly pack assemble for management review'], biz: 'Job-level costing (parts + labour + overhead applied) reveals which service lines actually make money. Budget-vs-actual by branch with variance reasons is monthly hygiene for any ISO-certified management review.', b: 'Decisions run on numbers everyone trusts; loss-making services get fixed or killed.' },
  /* A09 People & Workforce */
  M21: { p: 'Core HR: employee master, employment contracts, org structure, lifecycle events (hire → transfer → exit).', how: ['Every staff member is one employee record with contract, grade and org position', 'Lifecycle events (probation end, transfer, promotion) are dated records — history never overwritten', 'Org chart derives live from positions', 'Documents (contracts, NIDs, certificates) attach with expiry alerts'], biz: 'Position-based org design separates the role from the person — transfers stop breaking approvals and payroll. Statutory employment records (contract, ID) live in one auditable place.', b: 'HR answers any question about any employee from one screen.' },
  M55: { p: 'Time, attendance and workforce scheduling: shifts, rosters, field check-ins and leave.', how: ['Build branch/field rosters by week with skill requirements', 'Field staff check in to jobs (GPS-stamped) — attendance and job time in one event', 'Leave requests route to managers; balances update live', 'Exceptions (late, no-show, missed check-in) queue for review'], biz: 'Rostering to demand (forecast workload from M43/M26) is how service companies avoid both overtime bleed and SLA misses. Job-linked check-ins eliminate separate timesheet fiction.', b: 'The right people are scheduled where work will be; payroll hours come from reality.' },
  M56: { p: 'Payroll and statutory: salary structures, monthly runs, PF and tax deductions, festival bonus, payslips.', how: ['Maintain salary components per grade with statutory rules (PF, tax-at-source)', 'Monthly run pulls attendance and incentive data automatically', 'Review the pre-payroll exception report (missing attendance, bank details)', 'Payslips publish; GL posting file flows to M19'], biz: 'Bangladesh payroll carries statutory shapes: provident fund, income-tax deduction at source, festival bonus. Automation removes the classic double-pay/missed-overtime errors that destroy trust.', b: 'Payday is boring — accurate, on time, with a full audit trail.' },
  M57: { p: 'Performance and incentives: goal setting, review cycles, multi-source feedback and incentive computation.', how: ['Cascade company KPIs to team and individual goals per quarter', 'Review cycles run on schedule with self, manager and peer input', 'Incentive schemes compute from system data (closed jobs, CSAT, upsell, collection)', 'Calibration sessions normalise ratings across teams'], biz: 'Incentives paid from ERP data (not manager memory) are defensible and motivating. Multi-source feedback fits the cross-functional reality of service operations.', b: 'People see exactly how performance turns into pay — fairness you can audit.' },
  M22: { p: 'Learning and certification: course catalogue, enrollments, exams, certificate expiry tracking.', how: ['Browse the technical course catalogue by device family and skill level', 'Enroll in scheduled sessions or e-learning paths', 'Pass exams → certificates attach to the employee record and skills profile', 'Expiry alerts feed dispatch eligibility (M02)'], biz: 'Certification currency is a service-quality control: expired-certificate technicians doing warranty work can void manufacturer reimbursement. Skill data drives the scoring in dispatch.', b: 'Skills stay current by system design, and careers progress visibly.' },
  M58: { p: 'Talent acquisition: requisitions, sourcing, screening, interviews and offers.', how: ['Raise a requisition with budgeted position and justification', 'Pipeline candidates through screening and interview rounds with scorecards', 'Offers route for approvals; acceptance triggers onboarding in M21', 'Track source effectiveness and time-to-fill'], biz: 'Technician hiring is the growth bottleneck in BD after-sales; structured scorecards beat gut feel and reduce early attrition. Time-to-fill by branch feeds workforce planning.', b: 'Hiring is fast, structured and traceable — managers get choice, not chaos.' },
  M59: { p: 'Health, safety and wellbeing: incident reporting, safety audits, occupational health and wellbeing programmes.', how: ['Report incidents/near-misses from any device — photos and people involved', 'Safety audits run per site with findings and closure actions', 'Occupational health records (vaccinations, screenings for field/bench staff) track confidentially', 'Wellbeing programmes track participation'], biz: 'Electrical and battery work carries real hazards; incident statistics and closed actions are also tender requirements for corporate clients. Confidentiality of health data is a legal duty.', b: 'Safer shops, fewer incidents, and proof of care when clients ask.' },
  /* A10 Quality & Governance */
  M23: { p: 'ISO 9001 quality management: process documentation, non-conformances, CAPA, internal audits and management review.', how: ['Processes live as controlled documents linked to the modules that execute them', 'Raise a non-conformance (from complaints M34, QC gates, incidents) with severity', 'CAPA records root cause, corrective action, owner and verification', 'Internal audit plans schedule per process; findings flow back as NCs', 'Management review packs assemble automatically from quality data'], biz: 'ISO 9001:2015 — the certificate 1000 FiX alone holds in this sector — demands documented processes, corrective action discipline and evidence-based management review. The QMS makes certification a by-product of daily work.', b: 'Certification audits stop being terrifying; quality problems get fixed at root cause.' },
  M60: { p: 'Governance, risk and compliance: risk register, policy management, conflicts of interest, whistleblowing.', how: ['Maintain the risk register with owners, likelihood/impact and treatment plans', 'Policies publish with mandatory acknowledgement tracking', 'Declare conflicts of interest annually', 'Whistleblowing channel routes to independent review'], biz: 'Corporate clients audit their vendors\u2019 GRC posture; a live risk register is evidence. Policy acknowledgement records prove the "tone at the top" when incidents occur.', b: 'Risks are managed visibly; enterprise clients trust the governance story.' },
  M24: { p: 'Document management and e-signature: central library, versioning, templates and legally signed documents.', how: ['All controlled documents live in the library with metadata and retention rules', 'Templates (job sheets, contracts, NDAs) merge live data', 'Sign digitally — audit trail captures who signed what, when and from where', 'Superseded versions archive read-only'], biz: 'Under the Digital Signature Act context, e-signed documents with complete audit trails carry legal weight in disputes. Version control ends the "which contract version?" problem.', b: 'Every document is current, signed and findable — instantly.' },
  M61: { p: 'Knowledge and technical library: repair guides, fault codes, known-good diagnostics, expert Q&A.', how: ['Search the library by symptom, model or fault code before diagnosing', 'Technicians publish fix write-ups after novel repairs — reviewed before publishing', 'Known-good diagnostics shorten repeat cases', 'Usage analytics show which articles help first-time fixes'], biz: 'Repair knowledge walks out the door with senior technicians unless captured. A curated fix library compounds: every solved mystery makes the next one faster.', b: 'New technicians become productive in weeks; rare faults are fixed once, known forever.' },
  /* A11 Insights & Intelligence */
  M25: { p: 'Business intelligence: governed dashboards, self-service exploration and scheduled report distribution.', how: ['Open role dashboards (executive, branch, service line) — every metric has a certified definition', 'Drill from any chart to the underlying records', 'Build personal views from certified datasets (no SQL)', 'Subscribe to scheduled distributions (daily branch flash, weekly management pack)'], biz: 'One-number-one-definition governance ends dashboard anarchy. Certified datasets with lineage mean people trust and use the numbers.', b: 'Meetings argue about actions, not about whose spreadsheet is right.' },
  M26: { p: 'AI/ML services: the 14-model portfolio — demand forecast, dispatch optimisation, churn, price leakage, anomaly detection and more.', how: ['Consume model outputs where you work: the dispatch board ranks by predicted duration; churn scores appear in CRM', 'Review model performance monitors (accuracy, drift) monthly', 'Request new scoring via the model catalogue', 'Explanations accompany predictions (which factors drove the score)'], biz: 'Models earn trust through explanation and monitoring, not magic. Dispatch-duration prediction directly improves assignment quality; churn scores focus retention spend.', b: 'The system suggests the better choice — people decide with superpowers.' },
  M62: { p: 'Data platform and governance: pipelines, quality rules, catalogues, lineage and privacy controls.', how: ['Pipelines ingest from modules and external sources on schedule with SLAs', 'Data-quality rules run continuously; failures alert data stewards', 'The catalogue documents every dataset, owner and classification level', 'Privacy controls enforce purpose-limited access to personal data'], biz: 'Garbage-in is the death of ERP analytics; ownership plus quality SLAs keep the platform trustworthy. Personal-data classification supports Bangladesh\u2019s data-protection expectations.', b: 'Numbers across reports finally agree, because the data underneath does.' },
  M63: { p: 'Process automation: event-driven rules, approval routing, robotic task execution and exception queues.', how: ['Designers compose automations: when X happens → do Y → notify Z', 'Bots execute high-volume steps (invoice assembly, dunning emails, report distribution)', 'Exceptions route to human queues with full context', 'Every automation logs runs and outcomes'], biz: 'Automation ROI lives in high-frequency clerical steps. Human-in-the-loop exception queues keep judgement where it belongs.', b: 'People spend time on judgement calls; robots do the copying.' },
  /* A12 Platform & Technology */
  M28: { p: 'Integration, API and events: the governed connection layer for bKash/Nagad, couriers, e-commerce, government and partners.', how: ['External systems connect via the API gateway with credentials and rate limits', 'Event streams carry business happenings (order created, invoice issued) to subscribers', 'Integration monitors show message success/failure with replay', 'New connections follow the adapter pattern — no point-to-point wiring'], biz: 'Every payment rail (bKash, Nagad, SSLCommerz), courier and storefront integration flows through one governed layer — security, monitoring and replay in one place.', b: 'Integrations fail loudly, retry safely, and get fixed in minutes.' },
  M29: { p: 'Identity, access and security: accounts, roles, MFA, permission reviews and audit logs.', how: ['Users authenticate with MFA (TOTP like your login) — shared accounts are blocked', 'Roles bundle permissions by job; users get roles, never raw rights', 'Periodic access reviews require managers to re-certify', 'Audit logs answer "who did what when" for any record'], biz: 'Role-based access is both an ISO control and a fraud deterrent; the immutable audit trail is what makes evidence-based dispute handling possible.', b: 'Everyone sees exactly what their job needs — no more, no less.' },
  M30: { p: 'Platform administration: environments, configuration, monitoring, background jobs and system health.', how: ['Administrators manage configuration (numbering, tax rates, SLA calendars) — no code', 'Background jobs (billing runs, statements, distributions) schedule with monitors', 'System health dashboards watch performance and errors', 'Changes deploy through tracked releases with rollback'], biz: 'Configuration-over-code keeps the ERP adjustable by trained admins instead of developers; job monitoring catches silent failures before business feels them.', b: 'The platform is boringly reliable, and changes never arrive unannounced.' },
  M64: { p: 'Master data management: golden records for parties, items, assets with match/merge workflow.', how: ['Suspect duplicates queue for data stewards with match scores', 'Merge creates one golden record; histories re-link automatically', 'Standards (naming, phone format, BIN validation) enforce at entry', 'Quality scores show master-data health by domain'], biz: 'Masters decay without stewardship — duplicates multiply, prices split. Match-merge with survivorship rules is the enterprise answer.', b: 'Reports, loyalty and warranties stop breaking because "same customer, two records" ended.' },
  M65: { p: 'Multi-entity and localisation: companies within the group, parallel books, currency and language.', how: ['Operate entities (1000 FiX Ltd + future subsidiaries) with shared masters', 'Localisation packs carry language, VAT rules, number formats', 'Intercompany transactions price and settle automatically', 'Consolidated reporting rolls up with eliminations'], biz: 'Growth paths (new subsidiaries, exports) need entity-aware finance from day one — retrofitting entities is the classic ERP heartbreak.', b: 'The group can add a company without changing systems.' },
  M66: { p: 'Internal IT service: employee IT requests, incident management and the internal asset fleet.', how: ['Employees raise IT tickets (laptop, access, software) through the service portal', 'Incidents route to IT with SLAs and a knowledge base', 'Internal asset loans track custody', 'Recurring issues feed the problem registry'], biz: 'Eating your own cooking: the same service-management disciplines 1000 FiX sells, applied internally.', b: 'Staff IT problems get fixed fast — same discipline we sell.' },
  M67: { p: 'Projects and portfolio: internal change projects (ERP rollout, branch openings) with tasks, milestones and budgets.', how: ['Projects define scope, milestones, budget and manager', 'Tasks assign with dependencies; the board shows health at a glance', 'Timesheets and costs post to the project (WBS) — actuals vs budget visible', 'Stage gates require sign-off before the next phase'], biz: 'Internal change (like this ERP rollout) succeeds with the same discipline as customer work: scope, budget, gates. Actuals-from-system end status-report optimism.', b: 'Big changes land on time because progress is measured, not narrated.' }
};

module.exports = { AREAS, MODULES };
